WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.994
Hi, I'm Beet!

00:00:02.994 --> 00:00:07.272
Qwen3.8-Max has two point four trillion parameters, ninety-five billion active.

00:00:07.272 --> 00:00:10.695
Its best evidence is what it built alone.

00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:16.711
Alibaba's bet: scale environments, one reward and a balanced data mix together,

00:00:16.711 --> 00:00:19.897
and competence lifts across several harnesses at once.

00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:26.854
Environments grow along independent axes, task, workspace and harness, so growth compounds

00:00:26.854 --> 00:00:29.195
instead of needing bespoke integration.

00:00:29.615 --> 00:00:35.060
One reward system unifies execution checks, rubric judging and agentic inspection, instead

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.782
of a pile of task-specific verifiers.

00:00:38.202 --> 00:00:43.076
And an online balancer evens every batch across task, difficulty, workspace and

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:45.919
harness, so gradient variance doesn't blow up.

00:00:47.342 --> 00:00:50.448
Case one: a command-line tool from an empty repository.

00:00:50.448 --> 00:00:54.590
An agent leases an issue from a state machine, and implements it.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:58.521
Every change runs build, unit, end-to-end and desktop checks.

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:00.250
Passing pull requests merge.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:03.197
Failures route back to the issue that started them.

00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:07.136
Feedback and its own test results return as new issues.

00:01:07.136 --> 00:01:12.062
About sixteen days in: two hundred and sixty-five commits, none merged by a human.

00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:14.922
Case two, a chip.

00:01:14.922 --> 00:01:18.790
Over about five hundred turns, it cut the gate count from eight

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.013
thousand two hundred and ninety-eight to six hundred and seventy-eight.

00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:28.711
The benchmark table is real, but its footnotes admit different harnesses and competitor judges.

00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:32.559
The case studies leave traces you can check: a public commit history,

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:34.844
and a chip taken through place and route.

00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:39.510
A commit trail and a benchmark table are different evidence.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.505
This release offers both, and says which is which.

00:01:45.442 --> 00:01:49.786
To recap: one reward across many environments, a self-fixing harness, and a

00:01:49.786 --> 00:01:51.234
chip that kept shrinking.

00:01:51.654 --> 00:01:53.591
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:53.591 --> 00:01:54.144
I'm Beet.

00:01:54.144 --> 00:01:54.421
Bye!
